#!/bin/bash
# airlock-envoy-cred: the credentials Envoy adds to plain-HTTP requests (site file: http <host>
# ... credential <name>). The secret comes from stdin or the operator's password store, never
# from the command line; nothing here prints one.
#
# Each credential has a backend: file (the default: kept in /etc/airlock-envoy/credentials,
# always usable) or pass (kept in the operator's pass store; Envoy has it only while unlocked,
# in a tmpfs, until the unlock expires; locked, its requests get 423 from Envoy itself).
#
#   airlock-envoy-cred set <name> [--format bearer|token|raw|basic:<user>] [--file]
#       as root: a file credential. Read the secret from stdin (a prompt without echo on a
#       terminal) and make the Authorization value: bearer "Bearer <s>" (the default), token
#       "token <s>", raw "<s>", basic:<user> "Basic base64(<user>:<s>)". Envoy picks it up
#       within a second, no restart. --file turns a pass credential into a file one (its format
#       unless --format): the file is written, the config applied, and only then the pass
#       credential forgotten and its tmpfs copy locked; an apply that fails or is interrupted
#       leaves it a pass credential (the file locked if Envoy already reads it)
#   airlock-envoy-cred add <name> --pass <entry> [--format ...]
#       as root: a pass credential (the first line of the pass entry), locked until unlocked
#   airlock-envoy-cred migrate <name> --pass <entry>
#       as the operator, on the host: move a file credential to pass. Only once the entry gives
#       the same value: the config is applied (Envoy reads it from the tmpfs, locked) and then
#       the file is removed. Then unlock
#   airlock-envoy-cred remove <name>   as root: lock a pass credential and forget it; or remove
#                                     a GitHub App no applied line uses, once no token minted
#                                     with it is left unrevoked (it runs a mint first)
#   airlock-envoy-cred add-app <name> --app-id <n> --org <org>|--installation-id <n>
#                    --key pass:<entry>|file [--api <https url>] [--ca <file>]   (or add-github-app)
#       as root: a GitHub App, installed on <org> (its installation is looked up with the App's
#       own key, so setting it up needs no org admin; or name the installation). Its lines get tokens minted by
#       airlock-envoy-mint for their own repos and permissions. --key file reads the PEM key from
#       stdin (kept root:airlock-envoy-mint 0640); --key pass:<entry> unlocks it like any pass
#       credential (the whole entry is the key), held for the mint account only while unlocked
#   airlock-envoy-cred unlock [--for <n>h|<n>m]
#       as the operator, on the host (never in a box: it needs your pass store and sudo): read
#       the pass entries of the pass credentials the applied config uses, and hand them to
#       Envoy until /etc/airlock-envoy/unlock.conf's max_lifetime (default 10h, at most 24h),
#       its next expire_at (HH:MM, local time) or --for, whichever comes first. Unlocking
#       again needs the secrets again, and restarts the clock.
#   airlock-envoy-cred lock            lock every pass credential now (sudo as needed)
#   airlock-envoy-cred status          each pass credential: locked or unlocked, until when
#   airlock-envoy-cred revoke <name>   as root: replace a file credential with a value that
#                                     grants nothing (deleting the file would not: Envoy keeps
#                                     the last one)
#   airlock-envoy-cred list            as root: names, backends, formats, when each was set (and the cas)
#   airlock-envoy-cred ca <name>       as root: read PEM certificates from stdin, the only CAs
#                                     Envoy trusts for a host with "ca <name>" (a cluster's own
#                                     CA); prints each one's SHA-256 fingerprint to compare.
#                                     Envoy reads it at start: a change to one in use needs
#                                     systemctl restart airlock-envoy
#
# Run by the tool and its units, as root: entries (what unlock may read), receive (install
# what unlock read), receive-migrate, expire (the expiry timer), boot (before Envoy starts).
set -euo pipefail
umask 077
export LC_ALL=C
DIR=/etc/airlock-envoy/credentials
CA_DIR=/etc/airlock-envoy/ca
PASS_DIR=/etc/airlock-envoy/pass
UNLOCK_CONF=/etc/airlock-envoy/unlock.conf
ENVOY_YAML=/etc/airlock-envoy/envoy.yaml
RUN=/run/airlock-envoy-cred
SEC=$RUN/secrets
STATE=$RUN/state
TIMER=airlock-envoy-cred-expire.timer
APPS=/etc/airlock-envoy-mint/apps
MINT_CONF=/etc/airlock-envoy-mint/mint.conf
MRUN=/run/airlock-envoy-mint
MTOK=$MRUN/tokens
MKEYS=$MRUN/keys
MINT_USER=airlock-envoy-mint
MINT_SVC=airlock-envoy-mint.service
LOCKED=airlock-envoy-locked
APPLY_LOCK=/run/airlock-envoy-apply.lock   # airlock-envoy-apply's: remove refuses while one runs
EXPIRE_WAIT=60   # seconds the expiry waits for the lock before locking without it
die() { echo "airlock-envoy-cred: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
usage() { sed -n '2,/^set -euo/p' "$0" | sed '$d; s/^# \{0,1\}//'; }
self="$(readlink -f "$0")"
now() { date +%s; }
boot_secs() { cut -d. -f1 /proc/uptime; }   # CLOCK_BOOTTIME: runs through suspend, never steps back

[ $# -ge 1 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
cmd=$1; shift
case "$cmd" in -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;; esac

valid_name() { [[ "$1" =~ ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}$ ]] || die "bad name '$1' (a-z, 0-9 and -, starting with a letter)"; }
# A pass entry: /-separated parts of letters, digits and ._@+-, each starting with a letter,
# digit or _@+ (no option, no dot-file, no ..), at most 200 characters.
valid_entry() {
  local p; local -a parts
  [ "${#1}" -le 200 ] && [[ "$1" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._@+/-]+$ ]] && [[ "$1" != /* && "$1" != */ && "$1" != *//* ]] || return 1
  IFS=/ read -ra parts <<< "$1"
  for p in "${parts[@]}"; do [[ "$p" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_@+] ]] || return 1; done
}
# Printable ASCII without space, quote or backslash: what tokens are, and what goes into the
# SDS file's YAML string unescaped.
valid_secret() { [[ "$1" =~ ^[[:graph:]]+$ ]] && [[ "$1" != *'"'* && "$1" != *\\* ]]; }
header_value() {  # header_value <format> <secret>
  case "$1" in
    bearer) printf 'Bearer %s' "$2" ;;
    token)  printf 'token %s' "$2" ;;
    raw)    printf '%s' "$2" ;;
    basic:*) printf 'Basic %s' "$(printf '%s:%s' "${1#basic:}" "$2" | base64 -w0)" ;;
  esac
}
valid_format() {
  case "$1" in bearer|token|raw) ;; basic:*) [[ "${1#basic:}" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad basic user '${1#basic:}'" ;;
    *) die "unknown format '$1' (bearer, token, raw, basic:<user>)" ;; esac
}
duration() {  # <n>h or <n>m -> seconds
  [[ "$1" =~ ^([1-9][0-9]{0,3})([hm])$ ]] || die "bad duration '$1' (want <n>h or <n>m)"
  if [ "${BASH_REMATCH[2]}" = h ]; then echo $((BASH_REMATCH[1] * 3600)); else echo $((BASH_REMATCH[1] * 60)); fi
}

# The first line of the state, with the minutes left now (the state is written at unlock).
state_summary() {
  local dl
  dl=$(sed -n 's/^deadline //p' "$STATE")
  if [[ "$dl" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && grep -q '^unlocked' "$STATE"; then
    printf '%s; %s minutes left\n' "$(head -n 1 "$STATE")" "$(( (dl - $(now)) / 60 ))"
  else
    head -n 1 "$STATE"
  fi
}

# ---- as the operator, on the host -----------------------------------------------------------

# The pass store and its gpg key are the operator's, so these run as the operator and hand
# the secret to root through sudo, on stdin. A box has neither: no pass store, gpg key or
# agent reaches it (airlock refuses to mount them), and it has no sudo.
read_entry() {  # read_entry <entry>: its first line, checked
  local v
  valid_entry "$1" || die "bad pass entry '$1'"
  v="$(pass show -- "$1" | head -n 1)" || die "pass show $1 failed"
  valid_secret "$v" || die "pass entry $1: the first line must be printable ASCII, without spaces, quotes or backslashes"
  printf '%s' "$v"
}
read_pem() {  # read_pem <entry>: the whole entry, a PEM private key, as base64 on one line
  local v
  valid_entry "$1" || die "bad pass entry '$1'"
  v="$(pass show -- "$1")" || die "pass show $1 failed"
  [[ "$v" == "-----BEGIN "*"PRIVATE KEY-----"* ]] || die "pass entry $1 is not a PEM private key"
  printf '%s\n' "$v" | base64 -w0
}
case "$cmd" in
  unlock)
    [ "$(id -u)" != 0 ] || die "run unlock as yourself (it reads your pass store), not as root"
    for_arg=()
    if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
      [ "$1" = --for ] && [ $# = 2 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred unlock [--for <n>h|<n>m]"
      duration "$2" >/dev/null; for_arg=(--for "$2")
    fi
    list="$(sudo -- "$self" entries)" || die "could not ask which entries to read"
    [ -n "$list" ] || die "no pass credential is in use (add one, and apply the site file)"
    # A line each: <name> <the secret as base64> (a key is several lines).
    payload=""
    while read -r name entry kind; do
      valid_name "$name"
      case "$kind" in
        token) payload+="$name $(read_entry "$entry" | base64 -w0)"$'\n' ;;
        pem)   payload+="$name $(read_pem "$entry")"$'\n' ;;
        *) die "root named an entry of an unknown kind '$kind'" ;;
      esac
    done <<< "$list"
    printf '%s' "$payload" | sudo -- "$self" receive "${for_arg[@]}"
    payload=""
    exit ;;
  migrate)
    [ "$(id -u)" != 0 ] || die "run migrate as yourself (it reads your pass store), not as root"
    [ $# = 3 ] && [ "$2" = --pass ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred migrate <name> --pass <entry>"
    valid_name "$1"; valid_entry "$3" || die "bad pass entry '$3'"
    # Read first (pass may prompt), then sudo: root's side holds the lock only while it works.
    secret="$(read_entry "$3")"
    printf '%s %s\n' "$1" "$secret" | sudo -- "$self" receive-migrate "$1" "$3"
    secret=""
    exit ;;
  lock)
    [ "$(id -u)" = 0 ] || exec sudo -- "$self" lock ;;
  status)
    if [ "$(id -u)" != 0 ]; then
      [ -r "$STATE" ] || { echo "no pass credentials unlocked or locked on this host"; exit 0; }
      state_summary; sed -n '/^credential /p' "$STATE"; exit
    fi ;;
esac

# ---- as root ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[ "$(id -u)" = 0 ] || die "run as root"
[ -d "$DIR" ] || die "$DIR does not exist: run install/install-service.sh first"
[ "$cmd" != ca ] || [ -d "$CA_DIR" ] || die "$CA_DIR does not exist: run install/install-service.sh from this version first"
grp="$(stat -c %G "$DIR")"

# write <dir> <name> <header value> <format label>: an SDS file, swapped in atomically (Envoy
# watches the directory and takes the new file on the rename).
write() {
  local tmp
  tmp="$(mktemp "$1/.new.XXXXXX")"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2064  # the path now, not at exit
  trap "rm -f '$tmp'" EXIT
  {
    printf '# airlock-envoy credential %s, format %s, set %s\n' "$2" "$4" "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    printf 'resources:\n- "@type": type.googleapis.com/envoy.extensions.transport_sockets.tls.v3.Secret\n'
    printf '  name: "%s"\n  generic_secret: { secret: { inline_string: "%s" } }\n' "$2" "$3"
  } > "$tmp"
  chown "root:$grp" "$tmp"; chmod 0640 "$tmp"
  mv -f "$tmp" "$1/$2.yaml"
  trap - EXIT
}

# Pass credentials: $PASS_DIR/<name> (root only) names the entry and format. While unlocked,
# Envoy reads $SEC/<name>.yaml (a tmpfs, gone at reboot); locked, that file holds
# "airlock-envoy-locked <name>", which Envoy answers with 423 and never sends upstream.
# $STATE (world-readable, nothing secret) says which, and until when.
is_pass() { [ -f "$PASS_DIR/$1" ]; }
pass_field() { sed -n "s/^$2 //p" "$PASS_DIR/$1"; }
pass_names() { local f; shopt -s nullglob; for f in "$PASS_DIR"/*; do basename "$f"; done; shopt -u nullglob; }
run_dirs() {
  install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root "$RUN"
  install -d -m 0750 -o root -g "$grp" "$SEC"
}
# GitHub Apps: $APPS/<name> (app_id, installation_id, api, key pass|file, entry, ca). Their
# tokens are airlock-envoy-mint's; a pass key, unlocked, is $MKEYS/<name>.pem (the mint account's
# only). The Apps in use are those the installed mint.conf names.
is_app() { [ -f "$APPS/$1" ]; }
app_field() { sed -n "s/^$2 //p" "$APPS/$1"; }
apps_in_conf() { [ -r "$MINT_CONF" ] && awk '$1 == "token" { print $4 }' "$MINT_CONF" | sort -u; return 0; }
app_pass_in_use() { local a; for a in $(apps_in_conf); do is_app "$a" && [ "$(app_field "$a" key)" = pass ] && echo "$a"; done; return 0; }
# The mint's /run (tmpfiles.d makes it at boot): root's directory, root's keys/ and .lock; the
# mint account's tokens/, minted/, installations/. Root never writes in the mint account's: a
# link it planted there could steer root. Root's own parts are checked to be real directories.
mint_dirs() {
  local p
  for p in "$MRUN" "$MKEYS" "$MRUN/.lock" "$MTOK" "$MRUN/minted" "$MRUN/installations"; do
    [ ! -L "$p" ] || die "$p is a symlink: refusing (systemd-tmpfiles --create /etc/tmpfiles.d/airlock-envoy-mint.conf lays it out)"
  done
  install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root "$MRUN"
  install -d -m 0750 -o root -g "$MINT_USER" "$MKEYS"
  [ -f "$MRUN/.lock" ] || install -m 0640 -o root -g "$MINT_USER" /dev/null "$MRUN/.lock"
  # Below a root-owned directory: the mint account can't swap these for links.
  install -d -m 2750 -o "$MINT_USER" -g "$grp" "$MTOK"
  install -d -m 0700 -o "$MINT_USER" -g "$MINT_USER" "$MRUN/minted" "$MRUN/installations"
}
# The tokens of the lines in use, as "<token> <app>".
app_tokens() { [ -r "$MINT_CONF" ] && awk '$1 == "token" { print $2, $4 }' "$MINT_CONF"; return 0; }
mark_missing() {  # mark_missing <mint.conf>: each line's token file, locked or unavailable until minted
  local t a
  mint_dirs
  while read -r t a; do
    [ -f "$MTOK/$t.yaml" ] && continue
    if [ "$(app_field "$a" key 2>/dev/null)" = pass ]; then mark "$t" locked "$a"; else mark "$t" unavailable "$a"; fi
  done < <(awk '$1 == "token" { print $2, $4 }' "$1")
}
mint_tool() { local m; m="$(dirname "$self")/airlock-envoy-mint"; [ -x "$m" ] || m=/usr/local/sbin/airlock-envoy-mint; printf '%s' "$m"; }
# mark <token> locked|unavailable <app>: as the mint account (its directory, its files).
# Root's lock files are closed first: a copy held by a process of that account would hold them.
# A token's state (minted, locked, unavailable, missing), asked of the mint account: root never
# reads its files (a FIFO there would hang root holding the lock). Bounded by a timeout.
mint_state() { timeout 10 setpriv --reuid="$MINT_USER" --regid="$MINT_USER" --clear-groups --no-new-privs -- "$(mint_tool)" state "$1" 7<&- 9>&- 2>/dev/null | head -c 32 || echo unknown; }
mark() { timeout -k 2 10 setpriv --reuid="$MINT_USER" --regid="$MINT_USER" --clear-groups --no-new-privs -- "$(mint_tool)" mark "$@" 7<&- 9>&-; }

# The pass credentials the applied config uses: their files in $SEC are what it reads.
pass_in_use() {
  local n
  [ -r "$ENVOY_YAML" ] || return 0
  for n in $(pass_names); do grep -qF "path: \"$SEC/$n.yaml\"" "$ENVOY_YAML" && echo "$n"; done
  return 0
}
write_state() {  # write_state locked | unlocked <unlocked at> <deadline> <boot deadline>
  local tmp n
  run_dirs
  tmp="$(mktemp "$RUN/.state.XXXXXX")"
  {
    if [ "$1" = unlocked ]; then
      printf 'unlocked until %s (epoch %s); unlocked at %s\n' "$(date -d "@$3" '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z')" "$3" \
        "$(date -d "@$2" '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z')"
      printf 'deadline %s\nboot_deadline %s\n' "$3" "$4"
    else
      echo "locked"
    fi
    for n in $(pass_names); do printf 'credential %s pass %s\n' "$n" "$1"; done
  } > "$tmp"
  chmod 0644 "$tmp"; mv -f "$tmp" "$STATE"
}
lock_all() {  # lock_all [nowait]: nowait from boot (Envoy's start must not wait on a mint run)
  local n f
  run_dirs
  for n in $(pass_names); do write "$SEC" "$n" "$LOCKED $n" locked; done
  # And any other file there (a credential whose metadata went), so nothing stays unlocked.
  shopt -s nullglob
  for f in "$SEC"/*.yaml; do
    n=$(basename "$f" .yaml)
    grep -q "inline_string: \"$LOCKED " "$f" || write "$SEC" "$n" "$LOCKED $n" locked
  done
  shopt -u nullglob
  # Apps with a pass key: the key goes; a mint run in flight is waited for (it would put a
  # token back); their tokens lock now; a fresh run revokes them.
  [ -L "$MKEYS" ] || rm -f "${MKEYS:?}"/*.pem 2>/dev/null || true
  if [ -n "$(app_pass_in_use)" ]; then
    mint_dirs
    exec 7< "$MRUN/.lock"
    if [ "${1:-}" = nowait ]; then flock -n 7 || true; else flock -w 30 7 || true; fi
    while read -r t a; do
      [ "$(app_field "$a" key 2>/dev/null)" != pass ] || mark "$t" locked "$a" || true
    done < <(app_tokens)
    exec 7<&-
    systemctl start --no-block "$MINT_SVC" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  [ -z "${NOLOCK:-}" ] || return 0
  write_state locked
  systemctl stop "$TIMER" 2>/dev/null || true
}
# The unlock's end: unlock.conf's max_lifetime (or --for, never longer), and the next
# expire_at strictly after now, whichever is first.
deadline() {  # deadline [<--for seconds>]
  local life=36000 at="" key val t n
  n=$(now)
  if [ -f "$UNLOCK_CONF" ]; then
    while read -r key val || [ -n "${key:-}" ]; do
      case "$key" in
        ''|'#'*) ;;
        max_lifetime) life=$(duration "$val"); [ "$life" -le 86400 ] || die "$UNLOCK_CONF: max_lifetime over 24h" ;;
        expire_at) [[ "$val" =~ ^([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9]$ ]] || die "$UNLOCK_CONF: bad expire_at '$val' (HH:MM)"; at=$val ;;
        *) die "$UNLOCK_CONF: unknown setting '$key' (max_lifetime, expire_at)" ;;
      esac
    done < "$UNLOCK_CONF"
  fi
  if [ -n "${1:-}" ] && [ "$1" -lt "$life" ]; then life=$1; fi
  t=$((n + life))
  if [ -n "$at" ]; then
    # The day of n, local time; then the next day (date steps a calendar day, across DST too).
    val=$(at_on "$(date -d "@$n" +%F)" "$at") || return 1
    [ "$val" -gt "$n" ] || val=$(at_on "$(date -d "$(date -d "@$n" +%F) 12:00 + 1 day" +%F)" "$at") || return 1
    [ "$val" -ge "$t" ] || t=$val
  fi
  echo "$t"
}
# HH:MM on a day, local time; a time that day skips (DST springing forward) is an hour later.
at_on() {
  date -d "$1 $2" +%s 2>/dev/null && return
  date -d "$1 $(printf '%02d' $((10#${2%:*} + 1))):${2#*:}" +%s 2>/dev/null
}

# One mutating verb at a time (unlock, lock and the expiry must not interleave: a lock's timer
# stop landing after an unlock's start would leave secrets with no expiry). boot is a no-op
# without pass credentials, and then takes no lock (nothing to make in /run).
# What unlock and migrate send is read before the lock is taken, so a slow sender can't hold it.
# At most 60000 bytes (more is refused, not cut; and bash keeps a here-string that size in a
# pipe, never a temp file). A NUL becomes \001, which no check accepts.
input=""
case "$cmd" in
  add-app|add-github-app)   # --key file: the key on stdin, read before the lock
    case " $* " in *" --key file "*) [ -t 0 ] || input="$(head -c 20001)" ;; esac ;;
  receive|receive-migrate)
    input="$(head -c 60001 | tr '\000' '\001')"
    [ "${#input}" -le 60000 ] || die "over 60000 bytes on stdin: nothing was changed"
    [ -n "$input" ] || die "nothing on stdin: nothing was changed" ;;
esac
case "$cmd" in
  boot) [ -n "$(pass_names)" ] || [ -d "$SEC" ] || [ -s "$MINT_CONF" ] || exit 0 ;;&
  receive|receive-migrate|add|add-app|add-github-app|remove|lock|boot)
    run_dirs; exec 9> "$RUN/.lock"; flock 9 ;;
  expire)
    # The expiry must lock even if another verb holds the lock (fail closed): it waits a minute.
    # Without it, only the fail-closed steps (lock_all sees NOLOCK): the state and the timer are
    # left to the next expiry that gets the lock, so a stale one can't undo a later unlock.
    run_dirs; exec 9> "$RUN/.lock"; flock -w "$EXPIRE_WAIT" 9 || { NOLOCK=1; echo "airlock-envoy-cred: the lock is held; locking without it" >&2; } ;;
esac
cred_apply() {  # airlock-envoy-apply: next to this script in a checkout, else where install put it
  local f
  f="$(dirname "$self")/airlock-envoy-apply"
  [ -x "$f" ] || f=/usr/local/sbin/airlock-envoy-apply
  "$f"
}

case "$cmd" in
  entries)
    [ $# = 0 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred entries"
    for n in $(pass_in_use); do printf '%s %s token\n' "$n" "$(pass_field "$n" entry)"; done
    for a in $(app_pass_in_use); do printf '%s %s pem\n' "$a" "$(app_field "$a" entry)"; done ;;
  receive)
    forsec=""
    if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
      [ "$1" = --for ] && [ $# = 2 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred receive [--for <n>h|<n>m] < <name secret lines>"
      forsec=$(duration "$2")
    fi
    used=" $(pass_in_use | tr '\n' ' ')$(app_pass_in_use | tr '\n' ' ')"
    declare -A got=() key=()
    n=0
    while IFS=' ' read -r name b64 extra; do
      n=$((n + 1)); [ "$n" -le 64 ] || die "over 64 credentials"
      # A bad line is named by its number, never its text (it could hold a secret).
      { [[ "$name" =~ ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}$ ]] && [[ "$b64" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9+/]+=*$ ]] && [ -z "${extra:-}" ]; } \
        || die "line $n on stdin is not '<name> <base64>': nothing was unlocked"
      [[ "$used" == *" $name "* ]] || die "$name is not a pass credential or App key in use by the applied config"
      secret="$(printf '%s' "$b64" | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | tr '\000' '\001')" || die "line $n on stdin: not base64: nothing was unlocked"
      if is_app "$name"; then
        # A key openssl reads (from stdin: never a file of its own).
        printf '%s\n' "$secret" | openssl rsa -check -noout -passin pass: >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "the key for $name is not an unencrypted RSA private key: nothing was unlocked"
        key[$name]=$secret
      else
        valid_secret "$secret" || die "the secret for $name must be printable ASCII, without spaces, quotes or backslashes"
        got[$name]=$secret
      fi
      secret=""
    done <<< "$input"
    input=""
    for name in $used; do [ -n "${got[$name]+x}" ] || [ -n "${key[$name]+x}" ] || die "no secret for $name: nothing was unlocked"; done
    [ $(( ${#got[@]} + ${#key[@]} )) -gt 0 ] || die "nothing to unlock"
    # The deadline and the timer that enforces it come first: no secret is installed without them.
    t0=$(now); dl=$(deadline "$forsec") || dl=""
    bdl=$(( $(boot_secs) + ${dl:-0} - t0 ))
    [[ "$dl" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && [ "$dl" -gt "$t0" ] || { lock_all; die "could not work out the deadline (unlock.conf?): every pass credential is locked"; }
    write_state unlocked "$t0" "$dl" "$bdl"
    systemctl start "$TIMER" || { lock_all; die "could not start $TIMER (the expiry): nothing was unlocked"; }
    for name in "${!got[@]}"; do
      write "$SEC" "$name" "$(header_value "$(pass_field "$name" format)" "${got[$name]}")" "pass $(pass_field "$name" format)"
    done
    # App keys: for the mint account alone; then a mint run, which must give each of their lines
    # a token (a key GitHub refuses locks everything back).
    if [ "${#key[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
      mint_dirs
      for name in "${!key[@]}"; do
        # In root's keys/ (the mint account can read, not write, there).
        tmp="$(mktemp "$MKEYS/.new.XXXXXX")"
        printf '%s\n' "${key[$name]}" > "$tmp"; chown "root:$MINT_USER" "$tmp"; chmod 0440 "$tmp"; mv -f "$tmp" "$MKEYS/$name.pem"
      done
      # A run already going when the key landed may merge with this start: a second start then.
      # Minting is the network's pace: the lock is released meanwhile (the deadline and its timer
      # are in place), so Envoy's start, lock and the expiry never wait on it.
      minted_all() {
        local t a
        while read -r t a; do
          [ -n "${key[$a]+x}" ] || continue
          [ "$(mint_state "$t")" = minted ] || { bad_app=$a bad_tok=$t; return 1; }
        done < <(app_tokens)
      }
      exec 9>&-
      systemctl start "$MINT_SVC" 7<&- || true
      minted_all || { systemctl start "$MINT_SVC" 7<&- || true; }
      if ! minted_all; then
        exec 9> "$RUN/.lock"; flock 9
        key=(); got=(); lock_all
        die "app $bad_app: no token was minted for its line $bad_tok (journalctl -u $MINT_SVC): everything is locked"
      fi
    fi
    names="${!got[*]} ${!key[*]}"; got=(); key=()
    echo "==> unlocked $names until $(date -d "@$dl" '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z') ($(( (dl - t0) / 60 )) minutes): Envoy uses them from the next request"
    [ $((dl - t0)) -ge 1800 ] || echo "    note: under 30 minutes" ;;
  receive-migrate)
    [ $# = 2 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred receive-migrate <name> <entry> < <name secret line>"
    name=$1 entry=$2
    valid_name "$name"; valid_entry "$entry" || die "bad pass entry '$entry'"
    is_pass "$name" && die "$name is a pass credential already"
    is_app "$name" && die "$name is a GitHub App"
    [ -f "$DIR/$name.yaml" ] || die "no file credential $name"
    IFS=' ' read -r got_name secret <<< "$input" || true
    input=""
    { [ "$got_name" = "$name" ] && valid_secret "$secret"; } || die "the secret for $name must be printable ASCII, without spaces, quotes or backslashes"
    fmt=$(sed -n '1s/^# airlock-envoy credential [^,]*, format \([^,]*\), set .*$/\1/p' "$DIR/$name.yaml")
    case "$fmt" in bearer|token|raw|basic:*) ;; *) die "$name has format '$fmt' (revoked?): set it, or add it as a pass credential" ;; esac
    have=$(sed -n 's/^  generic_secret: { secret: { inline_string: "\(.*\)" } }$/\1/p' "$DIR/$name.yaml")
    [ "$(header_value "$fmt" "$secret")" = "$have" ] || die "pass entry $entry does not give $name's value: nothing was changed"
    install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root "$PASS_DIR"
    printf 'entry %s\nformat %s\n' "$entry" "$fmt" > "$PASS_DIR/.$name.new"; mv -f "$PASS_DIR/.$name.new" "$PASS_DIR/$name"
    run_dirs; write "$SEC" "$name" "$LOCKED $name" locked
    { [ -f "$STATE" ] && grep -q '^unlocked' "$STATE"; } || write_state locked
    # Envoy keeps the file's value until the config reads the tmpfs: apply first (without this
    # lock: Envoy's start runs boot, which takes it), and only then remove the file.
    exec 9>&-
    if ! cred_apply; then
      exec 9> "$RUN/.lock"; flock 9
      rm -f "$PASS_DIR/$name"
      die "the apply failed: $name stays a file credential; fix the apply and migrate again (if the apply got as far as Envoy, apply again once it works)"
    fi
    rm -f "$DIR/$name.yaml"
    echo "==> $name is a pass credential now ($entry), applied and locked. Next: airlock-envoy-cred unlock (as yourself)" ;;
  remove)
    [ $# = 1 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred remove <name>"
    valid_name "$1"
    if is_app "$1"; then
      # No apply at the same time (it could install a mint.conf naming the App, or check it).
      exec 6>> "$APPLY_LOCK"
      flock -n 6 || die "an airlock-envoy-apply is running: try again (nothing was changed)"
      ! apps_in_conf | grep -qxF -- "$1" || die "app $1 is in use: take its lines out of the site file and apply first (nothing was changed)"
      # Its tokens are revoked with its definition: a run first sweeps what is still recorded
      # (lines gone), at the network's pace and without this lock, so lock, boot and the
      # expiry never wait on it; then, under both locks, the mint account says whether any is left.
      exec 9>&-
      systemctl start "$MINT_SVC" 2>/dev/null || true
      exec 9> "$RUN/.lock"; flock 9
      mint_dirs
      exec 7< "$MRUN/.lock"
      flock -w 30 7 || die "a mint run holds its lock: try again (nothing was changed)"
      ! apps_in_conf | grep -qxF -- "$1" || die "app $1 is in use: take its lines out of the site file and apply first (nothing was changed)"
      rc=0; left=$(timeout 10 setpriv --reuid="$MINT_USER" --regid="$MINT_USER" --clear-groups --no-new-privs -- "$(mint_tool)" forget "$1" 6>&- 7<&- 9>&- 2>/dev/null | head -c 32) || rc=$?
      case "$rc:$left" in
        0:0) ;;
        1:[1-9]*) die "app $1: $left token(s) minted with it not revoked yet (GitHub unreachable? journalctl -u airlock-envoy-mint); the mint timer retries: remove it again later (nothing was changed)" ;;
        *) die "app $1: the mint account could not say what is left to revoke (nothing was changed)" ;;
      esac
      k=$(app_field "$1" key)
      rm -f "${APPS:?}/$1" "$APPS/$1.pem"
      [ -L "$MKEYS" ] || rm -f "${MKEYS:?}/$1.pem"
      exec 7<&- 6>&-
      echo "==> app $1 removed, nothing minted with it left unrevoked$([ "$k" != pass ] || echo " (its key stays in pass)")"
      exit 0
    fi
    is_pass "$1" || die "no pass credential or App $1"
    write "$SEC" "$1" "$LOCKED $1" locked
    rm -f "$PASS_DIR/$1"
    echo "==> $1 locked and forgotten (its entry stays in pass). Take it out of the site file and apply" ;;
  add)
    [ $# -ge 3 ] && [ "$2" = --pass ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred add <name> --pass <entry> [--format <format>]"
    name=$1 entry=$3; shift 3
    valid_name "$name"; valid_entry "$entry" || die "bad pass entry '$entry'"
    fmt=bearer
    if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then [ "$1" = --format ] && [ $# = 2 ] || die "unknown arguments: $*"; fmt=$2; fi
    valid_format "$fmt"
    [ ! -f "$DIR/$name.yaml" ] || die "$name is a file credential: airlock-envoy-cred migrate $name --pass $entry (as yourself)"
    ! is_app "$name" || die "$name is a GitHub App"
    install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root "$PASS_DIR"
    printf 'entry %s\nformat %s\n' "$entry" "$fmt" > "$PASS_DIR/.$name.new"; mv -f "$PASS_DIR/.$name.new" "$PASS_DIR/$name"
    run_dirs; write "$SEC" "$name" "$LOCKED $name" locked
    { [ -f "$STATE" ] && grep -q '^unlocked' "$STATE"; } || write_state locked
    echo "==> $name: pass entry $entry ($fmt), locked. Use it in the site file and apply, then airlock-envoy-cred unlock (as yourself)" ;;
  add-app|add-github-app)
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred add-app <name> --app-id <n> --org <org>|--installation-id <n> --key pass:<entry>|file [--api <url>] [--ca <file>]"
    name=$1; shift; valid_name "$name"
    appid="" inst="" org="" keyarg="" api=https://api.github.com ca=""
    while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
      [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"
      case "$1" in
        --app-id) appid=$2 ;; --installation-id) inst=$2 ;; --org) org=$2 ;; --key) keyarg=$2 ;; --api) api=$2 ;; --ca) ca=$2 ;;
        *) die "unknown option $1" ;;
      esac
      shift 2
    done
    [[ "$appid" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,19}$ ]] || die "bad --app-id '$appid'"
    [ -n "$inst$org" ] || die "--org <org> (the installation is looked up) or --installation-id <n>"
    [ -z "$inst" ] || [[ "$inst" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,19}$ ]] || die "bad --installation-id '$inst'"
    [ -z "$org" ] || [[ "$org" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9-]{0,38})$ ]] || die "bad --org '$org'"
    [[ "$api" =~ ^https://[a-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]{1,5})?(/[A-Za-z0-9._/-]*)?$ ]] || die "bad --api '$api' (an https:// URL)"
    api=${api%/}
    # Where the mint unit's sandbox can read it (no /home, /root, /tmp), and world-readable.
    [ -z "$ca" ] || { [[ "$ca" =~ ^/(etc|usr)/[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+$ && "$ca" != *..* && -f "$ca" ]] && [ "$(( 8#$(stat -c %a "$ca") & 4 ))" = 4 ]; } \
      || die "bad --ca '$ca' (a world-readable file under /etc or /usr)"
    { ! is_pass "$name" && [ ! -f "$DIR/$name.yaml" ]; } || die "$name is a credential already (file or pass)"
    id "$MINT_USER" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "no $MINT_USER account: run install/install-service.sh from this version"
    install -d -m 0750 -o root -g "$MINT_USER" "${APPS%/*}" "$APPS"
    case "$keyarg" in
      pass:*)
        entry=${keyarg#pass:}; valid_entry "$entry" || die "bad pass entry '$entry'"
        printf 'kind github-app\napp_id %s\n%s%sapi %s\nkey pass\nentry %s\n%s' "$appid" "${org:+org $org$'\n'}" "${inst:+installation_id $inst$'\n'}" "$api" "$entry" "${ca:+ca $ca$'\n'}" > "$APPS/.$name.new"
        rm -f "${APPS:?}/${name:?}.pem" ;;
      file)
        [ ! -t 0 ] || die "give the PEM key on stdin: airlock-envoy-cred add-app $name ... --key file < key.pem"
        tmp="$(mktemp "$APPS/.key.XXXXXX")"
        printf '%s\n' "$input" > "$tmp"; input=""
        if ! { [ "$(stat -c %s "$tmp")" -le 20000 ] && openssl rsa -check -noout -passin pass: -in "$tmp" >/dev/null 2>&1; }; then
          rm -f "${tmp:?}"; die "stdin is not an unencrypted RSA private key (a GitHub App's key is one)"
        fi
        chown "root:$MINT_USER" "$tmp"; chmod 0640 "$tmp"; mv -f "$tmp" "$APPS/$name.pem"
        printf 'kind github-app\napp_id %s\n%s%sapi %s\nkey file\n%s' "$appid" "${org:+org $org$'\n'}" "${inst:+installation_id $inst$'\n'}" "$api" "${ca:+ca $ca$'\n'}" > "$APPS/.$name.new" ;;
      *) die "--key pass:<entry> or --key file" ;;
    esac
    chown "root:$MINT_USER" "$APPS/.$name.new"; chmod 0640 "$APPS/.$name.new"; mv -f "$APPS/.$name.new" "$APPS/$name"
    echo "==> app $name (app $appid, ${org:+org $org}${inst:+installation $inst}, key ${keyarg%%:*}). Check it: airlock-envoy-mint check (once a line uses it). Use 'credential $name' on GitHub lines (repos named, one owner) and apply; a pass key: then airlock-envoy-cred unlock (as yourself)" ;;
  mint-mark-missing)
    # For airlock-envoy-apply: the token files of a mint.conf about to be installed.
    [ $# = 1 ] && [ -r "$1" ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred mint-mark-missing <mint.conf>"
    mark_missing "$1" ;;
  lock)
    [ $# = 0 ] || die "usage: airlock-envoy-cred lock"
    lock_all
    echo "==> locked: every pass credential answers 423 from the next request" ;;
  expire)
    # The expiry timer, every 30 s while unlocked: lock at the deadline, on either clock (the
    # wall clock may be set back; the boot clock never is, and counts suspend).
    # A state it can't read (missing, damaged, a deadline not a number) locks too.
    dl=$(sed -n 's/^deadline //p' "$STATE" 2>/dev/null || true); bdl=$(sed -n 's/^boot_deadline //p' "$STATE" 2>/dev/null || true)
